defines a Stack data structure (Last-In, First-Out) using a Python class and tests push, pop, peek, size, and is_empty.\",\n34:     code: `class Stack:\n35:     def __init__(self):\n36:         self.items = []\n37: \n38:     def push(self, item):\n39:         self.items.append(item)\n40: \n41:     def pop(self):\n42:         if not self.is_empty():\n43:             return self.items.pop()\n44:         return None\n45: \n46:     def peek(self):\n47:         if not self.is_empty():\n48:             return self.items[-1]\n49:         return None\n50: \n51:     def is_empty(self):\n52:         return len(self.items) == 0\n53: \n54:     def size(self):\n55:         return len(self.items)\n56: \n57: \n58: # Example usage\n59: s = Stack()\n60: s.push(10)\n61: s.push(20)\n62: s.push(30)\n63: \n64: print(s.pop())    # 30\n65: print(s.peek())   # 20\n66: print(s.size())   # 2\n67: print(s.is_empty())  # False`\n68:   },\n69:   binary_search: {\n70:     title: \"Binary Search (Two Pointers: left, right, mid)\",\n71:     defaultInput: \"arr = [1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13], target = 7\",\n72:     algorithmOverview:\n73:       \"This code searches a sorted list for a target number by checking the middle index (mid) and cutting the search range (left to right) in half.\",\n74:     code: `def binary_search(arr, target):\n75:     left, right = 0, len(arr) - 1\n76: \n77:     while left <= right:\n78:         mid = (left + right) // 2\n79: \n80:         if arr[mid] == target:\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":2186,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:42Z","input_tokens":3455,"cache_read_tokens":194694,"output_tokens":2768,"thinking":"The system includes a Python execution engine, an offline interpreter. It supports a comprehensive subset of Python features including basic data types, control flow, functions, classes, and scope management. This isolated environment provides a robust foundation for executing and inspecting Python code. Tracing capabilities allow for detailed runtime analysis, which may be useful.\n\n\nA dedicated learning page will be constructed to support problem understanding, pattern recognition, and hands-on coding. Initial focus is on presenting the problem clearly with examples and constraints, without immediately revealing the solution. The teaching section will incorporate rule-based content to introduce relevant DSA patterns, along with illustrative examples and explanations. The system will then provide a Python editor within a safe environment, integrating with the visualization tool for debugging.\n\n\nThe problem requires finding two numbers in an array that sum up to a given target. The output should be the indices of these two numbers.\n\nThe input is an array of integers (`nums`) and a target integer (`target`). The output is a list containing the two indices `i` and `j` such that `nums[i] + nums[j] == target`. I need to handle constraints on array length, value ranges, and edge cases.\n\n\nConsidering a brute-force approach, iterating through all pairs of numbers has O(n²) time complexity. Recognizing the need for efficiency, alternative methods are required. A hash table seems promising.\n\nThe hash table allows for O(1) lookup. The core idea involves checking if the complement (target - current number) exists. If it does, the indices are found.\n\nThis approach requires iterating through the array only once, achieving O(n) time complexity. The hash table stores each number and its index.\n\n\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\nA hash table appears optimal for efficient lookups. It requires storing each number with its corresponding index. I need to formulate the algorithm.\nThe algorithm will iterate through the input array, checking if the complement of the current number (target - current number) is present in the hash table. If the complement is found, the indices of the current number and the complement are returned.\nIf the complement is not found, the current number and its index are added to the hash table. This ensures each number's index is available for later checks.\nI'm checking the feasibility within the Python environment.\n\n\nA new subtab named 'workspace' will be added to the navigation.  The UI will include a \"Back to Problem Library\" or \"Back to Topic\" button, and within modals, there will be an \"Open Full Workspace\" button for navigation. The sidebar will dynamically display the \"Learning Workspace\" when a problem workspace is active. The system's test case execution mechanism will be examined by analyzing the provided code structure and example tests.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\code-flow-visualizer\\app_build\\dsa_problems.js","EndLine":150,"StartLine":50,"toolAction":"Viewing examples in dsa_problems.js","toolSummary":"View problem examples"}}]}
