rendering problems, providing teaching guides, and running user code with tests.  The plan involves implementing the problem workspace's core features by leveraging these methods, which handle problem loading, educational content generation, and code execution.\n\n\nExamining the code reveals the existing routing mechanism should directly open the dedicated learning workspace when a problem link is accessed, aligning with the new requirements. The option for a preview modal will be retained via an \"Open Learning Workspace\" button. Testing across existing test suites (`phase1` through `phase5`) is planned to ensure backward compatibility.\n\n\nExisting Summary:\nThe previous step computed and printed the value of `test_res` as `[0, 1]`. The agent can verify the computed value directly from the variables and the printed output. Any discrepancies will immediately indicate an issue with the code execution. Errors will also show in the trace object, allowing for targeted debugging if any arise.\n\nPlanning the dedicated problem workspace layout involves defining the `openProblemWorkspace` function to handle problem loading and routing. This function will set the active problem ID, current sub-tab, update the URL hash, and render the workspace components. The workspace's navigation header will include breadcrumbs and action buttons for bookmarking, reviewing, and solving, alongside a copyable link and visualizer access. The workspace itself will adopt a two-column, tabbed structure, with the left column dedicated to educational guides.\n\nIdentifying key edge cases such as empty input, single-element arrays, negative numbers, and equality among elements is crucial for robust algorithm design. The educational guide will feature a DSA pattern explanation contrasting naive and optimal approaches, highlighting efficiency gains. A step-by-step example and a concise approach will be offered alongside interactive practice tools like a code editor and run buttons.\n\nThe user interface's design now includes dedicated tabs for problem understanding, pattern learning, code editing with testing, and execution visualization. Checking for toggle functionality across these tabs ensures a flexible and intuitive workflow for users. The aim is a clean, uncluttered, and responsive interface by providing sub-tab switchers.\n\nAnalyzing the existing dashboard code reveals the required methods for workspace functionality, including those for opening, rendering problems, providing teaching guides, and running user code with tests.  The plan involves implementing the problem workspace's core features by leveraging these methods, which handle problem loading, educational content generation, and code execution.\n\nExamining the code reveals the existing routing mechanism should directly open the dedicated learning workspace when a problem link is accessed, aligning with the new requirements. The option for a preview modal will be retained via an \"Open Learning Workspace\" button. Testing across existing test suites (`phase1` through `phase5`) is planned to ensure backward compatibility.\n\nFocusing on the test suites, the plan is to verify the integration of new features within the current framework. The teaching content generator will leverage problem data fields to create tailored educational materials. Specific information extraction from each problem's data will enable the generation of the educational guide.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"# scratch/test_guide_gen.py\n# Tests the rule-based educational teaching guide generator\n\ndef generate_teaching_guide(p):\n    title = p.get('title', 'DSA Problem')\n    pattern = p.get('pattern', 'Algorithmic Pattern')\n    topic = p.get('primaryTopic', 'Data Structures')\n    summary = p.get('summary') or p.get('description', '')\n    \n    # Section A: Understand\n    what_is_asked = f\"The problem asks you to {summary.lower().rstrip('.')}. You must process the input and return the requested result meeting the specified time and space bounds.\"\n    \n    # What to return\n    if 'index' in summary.lower() or 'indices' in summary.lower():\n        what_to_return = \"Return the 0-based index or indices satisfying the target condition (e.g., [i, j]).\"\n    elif 'true' in summary.lower() or 'false' in summary.lower() or 'determine if' in summary.lower() or 'check if' in summary.lower():\n        what_to_return = \"Return a boolean value: True if the condition holds, False otherwise.\"\n    elif 'maximum' in summary.lower() or 'largest' in summary.lower():\n        what_to_return = \"Return the single maximum / optimal numeric value found.\"\n    elif 'minimum' in summary.lower() or 'shortest' in summary.lower():\n        what_to_return = \"Return the single minimum / shortest numeric value found.\"\n    elif 'list' in summary.lower() or 'array' in summary.lower() or 'group' in summary.lower():\n        what_to_return = \"Return the resulting list or array containing the calculated elements.\"\n    else:\n        what_to_return = \"Return the calculated answer matching the problem's expected output format.\"\n\n    # Edge cases\n    edge_cases = [\n        \"Empty or minimal input: ensure your code gracefully handles lists with 0 or 1 element.\",\n        \"Identical / duplicate values: verify whether the problem allows using the same element twice or requires distinct values.\",\n        \"Negative and zero values: check if calculations could encounter zero-division or sign inversions.\",\n        \"Boundary values: check if the optimal elements appear at the very start or end of the collection.\"\n    ]\n\n    # Section B: Learn the Pattern\n    why_useful = f\"A naive brute-force approach typically requires checking all combinations in O(n²) or O(2^n) time. The {pattern} pattern reduces time complexity to optimal {p.get('timeComplexity', 'O(n)')} by keeping track of essential state and avoiding redundant checks.\"\n    \n    small_example = f\"For a small input with 2 or 3 elements, {pattern} tracks key invariants step by step. When the target state is matched, it terminates immediately rather than scanning remaining unnecessary paths.\"\n    \n    intended_approach = [\n        f\"1. Initialize state: Set up necessary variables, pointers, or data structures for the {pattern} approach.\",\n        \"2. Iterate and update: Traverse the input once, updating your invariants and checking for the target condition.\",\n        \"3. Finalize result: Return the computed answer or fallback default if no match was found.\"\n    ]\n\n    # Hints\n    hints = [\n        f\"Hint 1 (Observation): What redundant work can you eliminate instead of re-scanning all elements? Think about what you already know at each step.\",\n        f\"Hint 2 (Data Structure): How can the {pattern} pattern or an auxiliary lookup structure help you achieve {p.get('timeComplexity', 'O(n)')} time?\",\n        f\"Hint 3 (Algorithm): Follow the invariant: maintain current state as you iterate, and check if the complement or boundary satisfies the goal.\"\n    ]\n\n    return {\n        \"whatIsAsked\": what_is_asked,\n        \"whatToReturn\": what_to_return,\n        \"edgeCases\": edge_cases,\n        \"whyUseful\": why_useful,\n        \"smallExample\": small_example,\n        \"intendedApproach\": intended_approach,\n        \"hints\": hints\n    }\n\nsample_prob = {\n    \"title\": \"Two Sum\",\n    \"pattern\": \"Hash Map Lookup\",\n    \"primaryTopic\": \"Arrays & Hashing\",\n    \"summary\": \"Find indices of two numbers that add up to a target using a dictionary for instant lookups.\",\n    \"timeComplexity\": \"O(n)\",\n    \"spaceComplexity\": \"O(n)\"\n}\n\nguide = generate_teaching_guide(sample_prob)\nimport pprint\npprint.pprint(guide)\n","Description":"Test teaching guide generator","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\CVT\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\4b927fe1-f9a3-4b3f-9f8a-92760e157eef\\scratch\\test_guide_gen.py","toolAction":"Creating test_guide_gen.py","toolSummary":"Test teaching guide generator"}}]}
